  "rebaselined": "2026-08-19",
  "reason": "Commit f2645a0 (2026-08-15), titled \"Refactor code structure for improved readability and maintainability\", contained an undeclared production retrain that moved these 30 artifacts. The checksum guard detected every one of them, which is what it exists to do, and five guard tests have been red since. These are the digests those fixtures were pinning BEFORE that retrain, preserved so the change remains auditable now that the fixtures track the current files. Re-baselining rather than leaving the tests red is deliberate and follows the 2026-08-11 precedent: a permanently failing guard cannot detect the NEXT change. This was held until the reproducibility study of 2026-08-19 had finished measuring the current artifacts, so the re-baseline pins files whose behaviour is known rather than merely current -- see DATA.md 8.1. What the re-baseline does NOT do is retire the underlying problem: the same commit also re-read the one-shot test block, which is disclosed in SUBMISSION_SELF_ASSESSMENT.md under Known limitations, and the recurrence is blocked separately at commit time.",
